PrepFlow
UI / UX Design System

Version: v1.0

Status: Draft

Author: Tejas Nere

1. Purpose

This document defines the complete design system for PrepFlow.

The purpose of this document is to ensure visual consistency across the entire platform.

Every page, card, button, progress bar, color, spacing, and interaction should follow the same design language.

2. Design Philosophy

PrepFlow is designed around one simple principle:

Students should spend their time studying, not understanding the interface.

The interface should always feel:

Clean
Modern
Professional
Minimal
Organized
Fast
Distraction Free
3. User Experience Goals

The platform should help students:

Understand where they are
Understand what they have completed
Understand what they should study next
Navigate without confusion

Every screen should answer only one question.

4. Design Principles
Principle 1

Reduce cognitive load.

Principle 2

Reduce clicks.

Principle 3

Reduce searching.

Principle 4

Show progress everywhere.

Principle 5

Consistency over creativity.

Principle 6

Every screen should feel familiar.

5. Theme

PrepFlow will use a modern dark theme.

The interface should look premium while remaining easy to read during long study sessions.

6. Color Palette

Primary Background

Dark

Secondary Background

Slightly lighter than the primary background

Card Background

Elevated dark surface

Primary Accent

Orange

Success

Green

Warning

Yellow

Error

Red

Text

White

Secondary Text

Light Gray

Borders

Subtle Gray

The platform should avoid excessive colors.

Only meaningful colors should be used.

7. Typography

Use clean sans-serif typography.

Font hierarchy:

Heading

Sub Heading

Body

Caption

Only three font weights should be used.

Avoid decorative fonts.

8. Spacing System

The interface should maintain consistent spacing throughout the application.

Cards, buttons, sections and lists should follow a fixed spacing system.

No random padding or margins should exist.

9. Navigation Philosophy

Navigation should always remain shallow.

Students should never navigate through deeply nested menus.

Ideal Navigation

Dashboard

↓

Subject

↓

Chapter

↓

Resources

No additional navigation levels should be introduced.

10. Dashboard Design

Dashboard should immediately show:

Overall Progress

Continue Learning

Subject Cards

Recent Chapters

Search

Analytics

Students should know what to do immediately after opening the application.

11. Subject Page

Each subject contains multiple chapter cards.

Each chapter card displays:

Chapter Name
Progress Bar
Completed Resources
Continue Button
Optional Metadata
Optional Tags

Cards should remain clean and uncluttered.

12. Chapter Page

The chapter page should display:

Chapter Information

↓

Progress

↓

Learning Checklist

↓

Resources

The interface should encourage sequential learning.

13. Resource Card

Every resource should have its own card.

Each resource card contains:

Completion Checkbox

Title

Description

Open Button

Optional Metadata

Students should never scroll through unnecessary information.

14. Progress System

Progress should exist at four levels.

Resource Progress

↓

Chapter Progress

↓

Subject Progress

↓

Overall Progress

Progress should update automatically.

15. Analytics

The dashboard should display:

Linear Progress Bar

Circular Progress Indicator

Subject-wise Progress

Chapter Completion Summary

Analytics should remain simple.

The platform is not a data analytics application.

16. Icons

Icons should be minimal.

Examples:

Playlist

Notes

Formula Sheet

PYQs

Practice

Completed

Bookmark

Search

Settings

Icons should always improve understanding.

Icons should never become decoration.

17. Buttons

Buttons should have clear purposes.

Primary Button

Main Action

Secondary Button

Alternative Action

Danger Button

Delete

Disabled Button

Unavailable

The number of button styles should remain limited.

18. Cards

Cards are the primary UI component.

Every important piece of information should be displayed inside a card.

Cards should contain:

Title

Content

Actions

Status

Optional Metadata

Cards should follow consistent spacing and alignment.

19. Status Indicators

Status should be communicated visually.

Completed

In Progress

Not Started

Draft

Hidden

Published

Students should understand status without reading long text.

20. Empty States

Whenever data is unavailable, meaningful empty states should be displayed.

Example:

"No Notes Available"

instead of an empty screen.

21. Loading States

The application should always display loading placeholders while fetching data.

Students should never see blank pages.

22. Search Experience

Search should be available globally.

Students should quickly search:

Subjects

Chapters

Resources

Future versions may include filters.

23. Mobile Responsiveness

The platform should be mobile-first.

Every screen should work smoothly on:

Mobile

Tablet

Desktop

Navigation should remain simple across all devices.

24. Accessibility

Buttons should be easily clickable.

Text should remain readable.

Contrast should remain high.

Spacing should prevent accidental clicks.

25. Admin Design Philosophy

The Admin CMS should feel like Notion.

It should focus on productivity instead of appearance.

Administrators should perform content management quickly.

26. CMS Principles

Everything editable.

Nothing hardcoded.

Everything configurable.

Everything manageable.

27. Future Design Expansion

The design system should support future features including:

Battle Mode

Leaderboard

Planner

Mock Tests

Community

AI Features

without redesigning the application.

28. Design Rules

Do not overload screens.

Do not use unnecessary animations.

Do not display excessive information.

Always prioritize clarity.

Maintain consistent spacing.

Maintain consistent colors.

Maintain consistent typography.

29. Final Design Goal

PrepFlow should feel calm.

Students should feel focused.

The interface should disappear, allowing learning to become the primary focus.

30. Design Statement

A good interface is one that students stop noticing because studying becomes effortless.